Cell["\<\
The implementation uses PolygonCoordinates and a fixed center {0.,0.}; use an \
origin-centered two-dimensional polygon whose vertices can be ordered by \
polar angle.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->705127906,ExpressionUUID->"c150b09e-b949-5546-80b1-28d30b28bcf4"],

Cell["\<\
nRadial should be a positive integer and angularScale must be positive. The \
origin itself is omitted.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->1588537914,ExpressionUUID->"1a605fa5-4bdb-5f4b-8a7f-9c633a762295"],

Cell["\<\
The radial construction returns nRadial + 1 loops: scales 1/nRadial through 1 \
followed by an additional outer-boundary loop. The two outer loops have the \
same radius but different sampling densities.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->1273352274,ExpressionUUID->"a5bc8465-db1a-0c4e-ac8e-f6bad06e9247"],

Cell["\<\
For loop index j, PathSample uses 1 + Ceiling[angularScale j] subdivisions on \
its shortest polygon side. Each loop omits the repeated closing vertex.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->1234998683,ExpressionUUID->"d5106e37-e2d2-254f-8da2-0b4156e5a9b9"],

Cell["\<\
The returned list of loops can be used for WannierChargeCenterByWilsonLoop. \
Nonconvex polygons or regions not centered at the origin require checking the \
actual sample geometry.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->917960765,ExpressionUUID->"c4671aa1-26de-664b-9036-de6342c35702"]
}, Closed]],

